{"id":"88a1182b-5d33-4933-93fc-57f2146bce74","object":"chat.completion","created":1784987858,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    refinement = case[\\\"refinement\\\"]\\n    edges = case[\\\"edges\\\"]\\n    members = {}\\n    for node, comm in refinement.items():\\n        members.setdefault(comm, []).append(node)\\n    for comm in members:\\n        members[comm].sort()\\n    agg = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        cu = refinement[u]\\n        cv = refinement[v]\\n        if cu <= cv:\\n            key = (cu, cv)\\n        else:\\n            key = (cv, cu)\\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\\n    agg_list = [[k[0], k[1], v] for k, v in agg.items()]\\n    agg_list.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\\n    return {\\\"aggregate_edges\\\": agg_list, \\\"members\\\": members}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for DATA-LEI-02. The output schema requires two fields: \"aggregate_edges\" and \"members\". The \"members\" object has dynamic keys from \"case.refinement.values()\", meaning keys are the values of refinement (community labels), and each value is an array of node strings that belong to that community. The \"aggregate_edges\" is an array of [string, string, number] tuples representing edges between communities (with sums of weights).\n\nGiven input case: has \"edges\" (list of [source, target, weight]) and \"refinement\" (dict mapping node -> community label). We need to produce:\n\n- members: dict where key is community label, value is sorted list of node names in that community.\n- aggregate_edges: list of [community_label1, community_label2, total_weight] for each distinct unordered pair (including self-loops) of communities that have at least one edge between them. The edges should be sorted (likely lexicographically by community labels, and tie-breaking?), also ensure that for each pair, the first community label is <= second? The schema doesn't specify ordering but typical aggregation sorts edges. We'll sort by (first, second) after ensuring first <= second.\n\nWe must copy keys from refinement values exactly, no parsing. So we get unique community labels from refinement.values().\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build community->nodes map from refinement dict.\n- For each community, sort node list.\n- Build aggregate edges: for each edge (u, v, w) in case.edges:\n    cu = refinement[u]\n    cv = refinement[v]\n    if cu <= cv: pair = (cu, cv) else pair = (cv, cu)\n    accumulate weight.\n- After accumulating, produce list of [pair[0], pair[1], total_weight] for each pair.\n- Sort the list by (pair[0], pair[1]) lexicographically.\n\nReturn dict with keys \"aggregate_edges\" and \"members\".\n\nMake sure to handle self-loops: if cu==cv, pair = (cu, cu) so self-loop edge weight is summed into that community's internal weight.\n\nCheck schema: \"aggregate_edges\" items must have exactly 3 elements: [string, string, number]. So number is float? But weights are integers? Use number, it's fine as int.\n\nMembers: additionalProperties with dynamicKeysFrom refinement.values(). So we just build dict.\n\nEdge cases: nodes may not appear in refinement? Assuming consistent. Could also handle missing nodes but not needed.\n\nNow produce Python code as a string inside JSON with key \"implementation\".\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". No markdown.\n\nWrite the solve function.\n\nNote: the case argument is the payload object directly. No case_id, seed etc.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    # Build members\n    members = {}\n    for node, comm in refinement.items():\n        members.setdefault(comm, []).append(node)\n    # Sort each list\n    for comm in members:\n        members[comm].sort()  # default string sort\n    # Build aggregate edges\n    agg = {}\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        cu = refinement[u]\n        cv = refinement[v]\n        if cu <= cv:\n            key = (cu, cv)\n        else:\n            key = (cv, cu)\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\n    # Convert to list of lists\n    agg_list = [[k[0], k[1], v] for k, v in agg.items()]\n    # Sort\n    agg_list.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"aggregate_edges\": agg_list, \"members\": members}\n\nThat's it.\n\nNow produce the JSON response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1486,"completion_tokens":1035,"total_tokens":2521,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":815},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":78},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}